  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses specifically on the most probable outcome of each lottery and prefers the one with the more favorable (higher) such outcome. In this case, Lottery B's most probable outcome (4000 Euros at 80%) is more favorable than Lottery A's (3000 Euros at 100%), so the reason would select B. The individual also chooses to go for 'more gain' (4000 Euros) and justifies it by saying the chance is 'high enough,' which aligns with attending to the 80% outcome as sufficiently likely. While the individual does not explicitly mention 'most probable outcome' or compare probabilities in a structured way, the focus on the higher gain and the acceptance of its likelihood as sufficient suggests an implicit reliance on the most probable, most favorable outcome. The reasoning is not perfectly articulated in terms of probabilistic dominance, but the core consideration—acting on the high-enough chance of the best likely outcome—matches the essence of the decision rule. Therefore, it is likely, though not perfectly explicit, that the individual used this reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 80
}

